@@ -0,0 +1,181 @@
+# Verified generator for arXiv:2605.05233
+
+> Status: the generator passes deterministic gates G1–G8 and the G9 size/effort
+> caps, but it is **not yet shippable**. The required OpenRouter runs produced
+> only HTTP 403 key-limit errors, so Step 4 and G9's oracle evidence remain
+> pending. No API error has been counted as a model failure.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | A — structural hardness |
+| Native domain | optimization |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | exact cover |
+| Certificate | integer tuple (a list of two-item bundles) |
+| Intuition | constraint propagation from global weight saturation |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 6 RN3DM-to-BMKP reduction |
+
+## Problem and trust
+
+The source is Chen et al., [*Near-Tight Approximation Algorithms for Bottleneck
+Multiple Knapsack Problems*](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.05233v1). An instance
+contains `n` arbitrary-capacity knapsacks and two distinguishable copies of every
+integer item weight from 1 through `n`; every item has profit one. The solver
+must assign exactly two items to each knapsack so that no capacity is exceeded.
+The answer is the allocation itself. Verification only checks IDs, disjointness,
+two integer additions per bundle, capacities, and unit profits.
+
+Construction is inverse, not solved: two random permutations are sampled first,
+their coordinatewise sums become knapsack capacities, and the corresponding
+item pairs are retained before item and knapsack labels are shuffled. Hence every
+generated instance has a certificate by construction. `verify` never reads the
+stored answer and accepts any valid allocation.
+
+The local evidence is reproducible and all non-oracle gates pass. The missing
+four-vendor transcript is a real trust limitation: this directory must not be
+submitted until a funded `OPENROUTER_API_KEY` reruns the three arms and G9 is
+updated from actual results.
+
+## Why Track A
+
+[Section 6](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.05233v1#S6) proves Theorem 3 by reducing
+strongly NP-complete RN3DM to precisely this unit-profit, arbitrary-capacity
+regime. A YES instance has optimum two; a NO instance has optimum one. The
+shipping distribution uses the reduction's `2n` items, two copies of weights
+`1..n`, `n` capacities, and target two. This is a claim about the generated
+distribution as well as worst-case hardness, so the theorem alone is not the
+evidence: MRV exact-cover search also failed on 8/8 fixed shipping samples after
+200,000 nodes each (1,600,008 calls and 63.264 seconds total).
+
+The easy-side results are Theorem 1 (identical capacities, a polynomial-time
+`2/3-epsilon` approximation for fixed epsilon) and Theorem 2 (arbitrary
+capacities, a polynomial-time `1/2-epsilon` approximation for fixed epsilon).
+Neither produces an exact target-two allocation. The generator avoids identical
+capacities and asks for the exact gap endpoint used by the hardness proof.
+
+The structural observation is that all `2n` unit-profit items must be used and
+their total weight equals total capacity. Therefore every capacity inequality is
+an equality, reducing arbitrary bundle search to complementary pair propagation.
+The module counts 240 exact additions/comparisons after this observation at the
+shipping preset.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=4, seed=3)`, the complete numerical data are:
+
+```text
+8 items (IDs 0..7), 4 knapsacks (IDs 0..3), every profit = 1
+W = [1,2,4,3,1,3,2,4]
+B = [3,5,7,5]
+Required output: one JSON pair per knapsack, using every item exactly once.
+```
+
+The planted answer is:
+
+```text
+<answer>[[0,1],[3,6],[5,7],[2,4]]</answer>
+verify(...) -> (True, "ok")
+```
+
+Dropping one item from its first bundle gives
+`[[0],[3,6],[5,7],[2,4]]`, and verification returns
+`(False, "wrong bundle shape: knapsack 0 needs exactly two item IDs")`.
+This demo is hand-solvable: list the pairs summing to 3, 5, 7, and 5 while using
+each displayed item ID once.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | n | Answer atoms | Role/status |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 4 | 8 | Hand example; exact enumeration gives 32 labeled witnesses for seed 3 |
+| easy | 32 | 64 | First oracle rung; oracle service failed before a valid attempt |
+| medium | 80 | 160 | Local construction/verification tested |
+| hard | 120 | 240 | Shipping candidate; all local adversaries failed |
+
+Escalation can move once to `n=126` (252 atoms); after that it returns
+`"cap_bound"`, because this native target-two witness necessarily writes `2n`
+item IDs and the corpus cap is 256 atoms.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted answers; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered through prose/fences; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware random allocations valid |
+| G5 | pass locally | shipping density 0/200,000; baseline 1,600,008 nodes / 63.264 s; demo exact count 32 |
+| G6 | pass | all four attacks 0/8, including MRV exact cover |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=240` instance builds and its plant verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 relabelling checks, 60/60 carried certificates, 20/20 distinct keys |
+| G9(c) | pass | 1,091 chars, about 273 tokens, 240 atoms, 240 intended operations |
+| G9(b) | **pending** | zero valid oracle attempts because all calls returned HTTP 403 |
+
+The structure-aware G4 prior already enforces what the statement gives away:
+every candidate uses every distinct item once and puts exactly two items in each
+indexed knapsack. Its candidate-space size is `(2n)! / 2^n`; the reported zero is
+an empirical upper signal, not proof that the true probability is zero.
+
+## Adversary panel
+
+| Attack | Successes |
+|---|---:|
+| Input-position/outlier pairing | 0/8 |
+| MRV greedy, most balanced feasible pair | 0/8 |
+| Randomized MRV greedy, 64 restarts | 0/8 |
+| Standard MRV exact-cover DFS, 200,000 nodes per seed | 0/8 |
+
+The random-restart attack traversed 55,065 partial assignments. Exact cover used
+the full node budget on every seed; its median wall time was about nine seconds.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The bare ladder stopped at `easy` because the service rejected all retries. The
+records remain in the script-owned transcript rather than being rewritten as
+failures.
+
+| Arm | Preset | Valid solved/attempts | API errors | Conclusion |
+|---|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | 4 | no hardness verdict |
+| hinted | hard | 0/0 | 4 | no hinted verdict |
+| placebo | hard | 0/0 | 4 | no placebo diagnostic |
+
+Thus hinted-minus-placebo is undefined. Once access is restored, rerun each arm
+in its isolated directory, copy the transcripts back, and replace the pending
+constants in `G9_ORACLE_RESULTS` only with the measured outcomes.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2605_05233 import make_instance, verify, DIFFICULTY
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+ok, reason = verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+From the repository root, after the oracle evidence is complete:
+
+```bash
+python3 results/2605.05233/gen_2605_05233.py
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2605.05233
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The paper proves worst-case inapproximability, not average-case hardness of these
+inverse-generated YES instances. The measured panel is the distributional
+evidence and covers only eight fixed seeds. A stronger exact-cover implementation,
+integer programming, SAT encoding, meet-in-the-middle method, or specialized
+RN3DM solver was not tested. Some other seeds can be easier, which is why the
+four-vendor hardening loop is mandatory rather than optional.
+
+Target two and total-weight equality are powerful clues; if pair propagation
+becomes deterministic on a particular draw, that draw is easy. Conversely,
+0/200,000 random candidates does not imply that heuristic or exact search has
+probability below that rate. Item IDs are shuffled independently, so position
+is not a planted signal, but the weight multiset is necessarily the paper's very
+regular two-copy construction. Finally, the intended-operation figure counts
+pair-sum validation after the saturation insight; it does not pretend that
+discovering a consistent exact cover costs only 240 operations.
